# Feature Implementation Strategy

This document outlines the comprehensive approach for implementing features in our Invoice Generator app using a frontend-first methodology before integrating with Supabase.

## Data Model Requirements

### Core Model Structure
- Create detailed data models that mirror all UI form fields
- Include all properties visible in listings and detail views
- Add relationship IDs to connect related models (clientId in invoices, etc.)
- Use appropriate data types for each field (String, double, DateTime, etc.)
- Include unique identifiers that will match Supabase primary keys

### Validation & Business Logic
- Implement validation rules for all user inputs
- Add helper methods for data transformation and formatting
- Include calculated properties (totals, subtotals, etc.)
- Create utility functions for common operations on the model
- Add data sanitization methods for user inputs

### Serialization
- Implement methods to convert models to/from JSON
- Include all fields that will be stored in Supabase
- Handle date/time formatting consistently
- Manage nested objects and relationships appropriately
- Prepare for future backend schema alignment

## State Management Approach

### Provider Implementation
- Create a dedicated provider for each major feature
- Implement a local in-memory collection to store items
- Add methods for all CRUD operations (Create, Read, Update, Delete)
- Include sorting, filtering, and search functionality
- Ensure the provider is accessible across the relevant screens

### State Operations
- Implement adding new items with generated temporary IDs
- Create update methods that maintain state consistency
- Add deletion with proper state cleanup
- Include methods to fetch items by ID or other attributes
- Implement filtering capabilities (by status, date range, etc.)

### UI Synchronization
- Ensure state changes trigger appropriate UI rebuilds
- Implement optimistic updates for better UX
- Add artificial delays to simulate network operations
- Create loading states for async operations
- Implement proper error handling and user feedback

## UI-State Connection

### Form Binding
- Connect all form inputs to model properties
- Implement two-way data binding where appropriate
- Add input validation with error message display
- Create form submission handlers that update the state
- Preserve form state during navigation

### List Displays
- Bind list views to the provider's collection
- Implement immediate UI updates when items change
- Add empty state handling when no items exist
- Create sorted/filtered views based on user selection
- Implement pagination if dealing with large datasets

### Status & Feedback
- Show loading indicators during operations
- Display success messages after completed actions
- Implement error messaging for failed operations
- Add confirmation dialogs for destructive actions
- Create status indicators for different item states

## Backend Preparation

### Architecture Design
- Separate data access logic from UI and business logic
- Create service classes that can be updated for Supabase
- Use repository pattern to abstract data sources
- Implement consistent async/await pattern for all data operations
- Structure code to minimize changes when switching to Supabase

### Future-Proofing
- Design with authentication in mind (userId fields, etc.)
- Prepare for real-time updates from Supabase
- Consider offline capabilities and cache handling
- Plan for conflict resolution in concurrent edits
- Structure for eventual server-side validation

### Error Handling
- Implement comprehensive error catching
- Create error types that map to potential API errors
- Add retry logic where appropriate
- Include fallback behavior for failed operations
- Design user-friendly error messaging

## Complete User Flow

### Navigation Management
- Implement proper screen transitions after actions
- Return to appropriate screens after form submission
- Preserve state during navigation
- Handle back button behavior consistently
- Create logical workflow between related screens

### Form Processing
- Validate all inputs before submission
- Show field-specific error messages
- Disable submission until form is valid
- Preserve partially completed forms when possible
- Implement auto-save for lengthy forms

### Feedback & Confirmation
- Add confirmation for destructive actions
- Show success messages after completed operations
- Implement progress indicators for multi-step processes
- Create toast messages or snackbars for important notifications
- Provide clear next steps after completing actions

### Edge Cases
- Handle empty states appropriately
- Implement error recovery mechanisms
- Add timeout handling for simulated network operations
- Create fallbacks for missing or invalid data
- Design for various device sizes and orientations

## Testing Considerations

### Functional Testing
- Test all CRUD operations thoroughly
- Verify form validation works correctly
- Ensure state updates properly reflect in UI
- Test edge cases (empty lists, invalid inputs, etc.)
- Verify navigation flows work as expected

### UI Testing
- Test responsiveness on different screen sizes
- Verify all interactive elements are properly sized
- Ensure loading states display correctly
- Test error message displays
- Verify empty states and data-populated states

### Persistence Testing
- Verify state maintains during navigation
- Test app restart behavior (if implementing local storage)
- Ensure proper cleanup of temporary data when needed
- Test filtering and sorting persistence
- Verify form state preservation works correctly

By following this comprehensive strategy, we'll create features that function completely on the frontend now while being structured for seamless Supabase integration later.